        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Polymeric sand's pros are real and lasting: full joints, far fewer weeds and ants, resistance to washout, and a clean finished look. Its cons are mostly about installation, which demands dry pavers, a dry forecast, a spotless surface and patient watering, plus a sound base and the right product for the joint width.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you can meet those conditions, it is almost always worth the extra cost on a paver patio or driveway. If you cannot, plain sand with regular upkeep is a reasonable choice, and you can upgrade later when the timing is right.
          </p>
